WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.290
I'm Gosia Trynka

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:15.250
I work as a group leader at the Sanger Institute in Cambridge.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.000
My group is interested in understanding how

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:28.369
genetic variants modulate the immune system and in particular how they make the immune system

00:00:28.369 --> 00:00:32.930
attack your own body, so that leads to
the autoimmune diseases

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:38.640
such as Type 1 diabetes or coeliac disease - gluten intolerance.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.940
I have a wet lab background, that's what I did

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.510
during my Master's studies

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:47.110
and then I went through my PhD and I was 
slowly understanding that I really

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:50.070
liked the computational part of the research.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.840
So for my next step for
my post doc I decided to make it

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.530
really purely computational so I spent
two years developing statistical methods

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:02.090
but at that point also I realised
that I was actually missing

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:06.189
the wet lab component of it and that's
really what brings me here

00:01:06.189 --> 00:01:10.100
and to my group where we have both the
wet lab and the dry lab

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.590
component really integrated that means
that we can

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:17.640
have research questions and address them
by generating our own data and

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.920
then analysing it ourselves.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:23.060
In the wet lab we're trying to
understand

00:01:23.069 --> 00:01:26.530
how the immune cells function and so for
that

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:31.189
we're isolating specific immune cell
types and then we're stimulating them to

00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:32.609
see how they respond.

00:01:32.609 --> 00:01:37.069
And then in order to measure that
respose on the molecular level

00:01:37.069 --> 00:01:41.899
we perform different genomic assays and
the output of those assays

00:01:41.899 --> 00:01:44.180
so the data that we generate in the wet lab

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:47.370
is then analysed in the dry lab.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:51.990
Alot of the computational work really
involves getting rid of the

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.090
background noise from the data, the
experimental noise,

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:00.270
and fishing out that biological signal
that is important to us

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:05.670
and focusing on that. I always get really
very excited when the Natalia comes in

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.220
with some results and I want to know how
she did it?

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.800
What did she change? What things are
working? What things are not working?

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.090
How are we going to change them?

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:18.570
It's the process itself and then the 
satisfaction when you get something

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:19.580
to work.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:24.360
It's definitely one of the best parts
about being a scientist,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.960
this moment when you go, yes it worked!

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.920
Or yes I found out
something new

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:30.210
It's amazing.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.060
I've never had a master
plan, I never aimed to be here,

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:38.940
I always followed my passion, my interest

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.040
and the science that made me excited.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:47.260
I never planned to do a PhD or become a
geneticist

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:50.730
so I don't know where I'm gonna be in
the future

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:54.670
but I'm also not worried about that
because I'm just going to keep on following

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.560
the research that makes me really excited.

